UsageMetadata: all three fields required (`inputTokens`, `outputTokens`, `totalTokens` — all `number`). So a reported usage always has all three fields per the type. Per-field `?? 0` handling then is defensive but the type says fields are present when the object is present. Hmm — but runtime providers could supply partial objects... The type contract says no. But the acceptance criterion says "A provider that reports no usage on some rounds does not make the total undefined or NaN" — "reports no usage on some rounds" = the round's stop chunk carries no usage (undefined), which acceptance test 2 covers. So NaN safety: with all-required fields, summing two defined objects can't produce NaN. The only NaN risk would be partial objects at runtime, which the type excludes.

